{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The dropdown that won't stay closed</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You build a dropdown menu. Click outside it, it should close — you wire up a click
      listener on <code>document</code> for that. It works, until a button <em>inside</em>
      another widget on the page calls <code>stopPropagation()</code> on its own click.
      Now clicking that button doesn't close your dropdown, even though the click was
      clearly outside it. Your listener never even ran.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A default <code>addEventListener</code> call listens during the bubble phase — the
      DOM walks from the clicked element back up to the root, firing listeners on the way.
      <code>stopPropagation()</code> called partway up that walk stops it dead: nothing
      further up the tree ever hears about the click, including a bubble-phase listener
      on <code>document</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Events don't just bubble — they capture first. The DOM dispatches every event in
      three phases: capture (root → target), target, then bubble (target → root).
      A listener registered with <code>{ capture: true }</code> runs on the way down,
      before the click ever reaches the element that calls <code>stopPropagation()</code>.
      That's the actual fix, and it's why most "click outside" implementations you'll find
      in real component libraries register on <code>document</code> with capture, not bubble.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Every click happens twice: down, then up</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Picture three nested boxes: Outer, Middle, Inner. Click Inner, and the browser
      doesn't just tell Inner about it — it walks the whole chain twice. First it walks
      <strong>down</strong> from the page's root to Inner (that's capturing), then it
      walks <strong>up</strong> from Inner back out to the root (that's bubbling).
      By default, <code>addEventListener</code> only listens on the way up.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>addEventListener(type, handler, options)</code>'s third argument controls
      which walk a listener hears: <code>{ capture: true }</code> (or the legacy boolean
      <code>true</code>) attaches it to the downward pass; omitting it, or passing
      <code>false</code>, attaches it to the upward pass — the default almost everyone uses
      without thinking about it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Order matters because it determines who sees the event first. With three nested
      capture-phase listeners, Outer fires before Middle fires before Inner. With three
      nested bubble-phase listeners, it's the exact reverse: Inner, then Middle, then Outer.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is defined by the DOM Events spec's event dispatch algorithm, not a browser
      quirk or a framework convention — capture-then-target-then-bubble is the same in
      every evergreen browser. <code>Event.eventPhase</code> reports which phase a
      listener is currently running in (1 = capturing, 2 = at target, 3 = bubbling), and
      <code>Event.stopPropagation()</code> halts whichever walk is currently in progress —
      it does not retroactively cancel listeners that already ran in an earlier phase.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>Why capture-phase "click outside" is the common pattern</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Open the dev tools on almost any dropdown, popover, or modal from a real component
      library and search its source for <code>addEventListener('click'</code>. You'll
      usually find a call ending in <code>true</code> or <code>{ capture: true }</code>.
      That's deliberate: the library doesn't want some other widget's
      <code>stopPropagation()</code> to be able to hide a click from its own
      "did you click outside me?" check.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Registering the outside-click listener in the capture phase means it runs before
      the DOM even reaches the element that was actually clicked — before that element's
      own bubble-phase handler has a chance to call <code>stopPropagation()</code>. The
      outside-click check has already happened by then; nothing downstream can suppress it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The tradeoff: a capture-phase listener on <code>document</code> runs on literally
      every click on the page, before anything else does. That's correct for "did this
      click land outside my element" checks (cheap: one <code>element.contains(target)</code>
      call), but it's the wrong tool for anything that should be skippable — a capture-phase
      listener can't be shut off by an inner element's <code>stopPropagation()</code>,
      which is exactly the point, but also means it can't be opted out of either.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

