{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Where this actually bites in real code</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      React's synthetic event system attaches most listeners at the document root and
      simulates bubbling for you — <code>onClick</code> in JSX is a bubble-phase handler.
      If you need capture in React, the prop name has a suffix:
      <code>onClickCapture</code>, <code>onMouseDownCapture</code>, and so on, for every
      event type that supports it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>{ once: true }</code> and <code>{ capture: true }</code> compose in the same
      options object — <code>addEventListener('click', fn, { capture: true, once: true })</code>
      is a valid, common combination for "run exactly once, before anything downstream can
      suppress it," e.g. dismissing a tooltip on the very next click anywhere on the page.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Removing a capture-phase listener requires matching the options: <code>removeEventListener</code>
      needs the same <code>capture</code> value used at registration (or it silently
      no-ops, doesn't throw). This is the single most common bug when wiring capture-phase
      listeners by hand — forgetting to pass <code>{ capture: true }</code> to the matching
      <code>removeEventListener</code> call leaves a listener attached forever, a real
      memory/behavior leak in any component that mounts and unmounts repeatedly.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

