{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">Every click walks the DOM down first (capture), then up (bubble). Regular addEventListener only listens on the way up.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Pass <code>{ capture: true }</code> as the third argument to listen on the way down instead — before any inner element's own handler runs.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Capture-then-target-then-bubble is defined by the DOM Events spec's dispatch algorithm — identical behavior across every evergreen browser, not a framework convention.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">A capture-phase listener can't be silenced by an inner element calling stopPropagation() — it already ran before that inner element was even reached.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">This is exactly why "click outside to close" implementations in real component libraries register on document with capture: true, not the bubble-phase default.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">stopPropagation() halts whichever pass is currently in progress; it cannot retroactively cancel listeners that already ran in an earlier phase.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">removeEventListener needs to match how the listener was added — if you registered with capture, you have to remove with capture too.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Mismatched capture options on removeEventListener don't throw — they silently no-op, leaving the listener attached. This is the most common real bug with capture-phase listeners.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">In React, capture-phase handlers are separate props (onClickCapture, etc.) — JSX's plain onClick is always bubble-phase, simulated at the document root.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
